---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro';
import { firstRun, evalDefinition, agentDefinition, fixtureDefinition, scorerDefinition, resultsCommand, projectConfig, matrixCommands } from '../../content/snippets';
---

<Site title="Get started — Evalkit" description="Run your first local agent evaluation and learn how to add agents, fixtures, scorers, and inspect results." active="docs">
  <div class="docs-header wrap"><span class="section-kicker">EVALKIT / FIELD GUIDE</span><h1>Get started<span class="text-accent">.</span></h1><p>Everything you need to run your first eval, then make one of your own. This describes the current local Bun implementation.</p></div>
  <div class="docs-layout wrap">
    <aside class="docs-sidebar" aria-label="On this page"><span>ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#first-run">01 / First run</a><a href="#create-an-eval">02 / Define an eval</a><a href="#agents">03 / Add an agent</a><a href="#fixtures">04 / Add fixtures</a><a href="#scoring">05 / Score a trial</a><a href="#results">06 / Read results</a><a href="#configure">07 / Configure</a><a href="#matrices">08 / Sweep parameters</a><a href="#next">09 / What’s next</a><div class="sidebar-separator"></div><a href="/docs/manual/">Full manual ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/leostera/evalkit/tree/main/examples/starter">Starter source ↗</a></aside>
    <div class="docs-body">
      <section id="first-run" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>01 / FIRST RUN</span><h2>Try the starter.</h2></div><p>Evalkit is currently a Bun monorepo. <code>bun create</code> copies the repository into <code>evalkit/</code> and installs its workspace dependencies. Then run the self-contained greeting eval from the starter project; it does not require Pi or a model provider.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">TERMINAL</div><Code code={firstRun} lang="bash" theme="github-light" /></div><p>The final command starts the dashboard and prints its local URL. Run it in a second terminal if you want to keep using the first one. <strong>Run commands from your eval project directory:</strong> that’s where Evalkit finds <code>src/registry.ts</code>, fixture paths, and the results folder.</p><div class="callout"><span>NOTE</span><p><code>bun run evals</code> runs the entire starter suite, including Pi-backed evals. Those require a working <code>pi</code> command and configured model access.</p></div><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#run-from-the-cli">Running from the CLI →</a></p></section>

      <section id="create-an-eval" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>02 / DEFINE AN EVAL</span><h2>Describe the task.</h2></div><p>An eval brings together an agent, user messages, fixtures, and scorers. Give it a stable, human-readable <code>id</code> such as <code>greeting</code>. Use lowercase kebab-case IDs for authored resources; Evalkit generates run and trial identities.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">evals/greeting.eval.ts</div><Code code={evalDefinition} lang="ts" theme="github-light" /></div><p>Register it explicitly in <code>src/registry.ts</code> with <code>export default registerEvals([greetingEval])</code>. Alternatively, default-export an eval from <code>evals/*.eval.ts</code> for discovery. Group related evals with <code>defineSuite(&#123; id, name, evals &#125;)</code>; run one with <code>run-evals &lt;eval-id&gt;</code> or a suite with <code>run-suite &lt;suite-id&gt;</code>.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#project-structure-and-registration">Project structure and registration →</a> · <a href="/docs/manual/#add-a-scorer-and-an-eval">Eval definitions →</a></p></section>

      <section id="agents" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>03 / ADD AN AGENT</span><h2>Wrap a session.</h2></div><p>An Agent Under Test (AUT) is an adapter with <code>start()</code>, <code>send(message)</code>, and <code>close()</code>. Each trial gets a fresh session. Your adapter emits events for the timeline and scorers — returning a value from <code>send()</code> alone is not enough.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">agents/greeting-agent.ts · essentials</div><Code code={agentDefinition} lang="ts" theme="github-light" /></div><p>Use <code>context.workspace.root</code> for candidate-visible files. Never pass <code>context.evaluatorWorkspace.root</code> to the model. For a local Pi process, <code>piAgent()</code> is available from <code>@evalkit/agents</code>, but invokes Pi independently for each message.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#add-an-agent">Adding an agent →</a></p></section>

      <section id="fixtures" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>04 / ADD FIXTURES</span><h2>Give it something to work on.</h2></div><p>Fixtures are copied into isolated workspaces before each trial. Candidate files are visible to the agent; evaluator files are private to trusted scoring code. Fixture source paths resolve from the directory where you invoke Evalkit.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">evals/greeting.eval.ts · fixture options</div><Code code={fixtureDefinition} lang="ts" theme="github-light" /></div><p>Add <code>fixtures</code> to the eval definition. The <code>dynamic()</code> helper can generate per-trial fixtures using the trial context and its random seed. Keep expected answers out of candidate files.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#add-fixtures">Fixture types and visibility →</a></p></section>

      <section id="scoring" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>05 / SCORE A TRIAL</span><h2>Make success explicit.</h2></div><p>A predicate examines events and files after the session closes. Return a score between 0 and 1. A numeric score passes only when it is exactly 1; return an object with <code>passed</code> to set your own threshold.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">judges/greeting.ts</div><Code code={scorerDefinition} lang="ts" theme="github-light" /></div><p>The callback also receives <code>artifacts.candidate.root</code> and <code>artifacts.evaluator.root</code> to inspect trial files. Score errors are saved, not silently treated as passes.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#add-a-scorer-and-an-eval">Scorers and evals →</a></p></section>

      <section id="results" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>06 / READ RESULTS</span><h2>Follow the evidence.</h2></div><p>Each run is saved under <code>_evalkit-results/&lt;run-id&gt;/</code>. Read <code>summary.json</code> for passed and failed trial counts; inside <code>trials/&lt;trial-id&gt;/</code>, inspect <code>trajectory.jsonl</code>, <code>scoring.json</code>, <code>summary.json</code>, and the candidate artifact snapshot.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">TERMINAL · replace the run ID</div><Code code={resultsCommand} lang="bash" theme="github-light" /></div><div class="callout"><span>IMPORTANT</span><p>Execution status <code>completed</code> does not mean the scores passed. For a CI gate, inspect the run-level <code>failed</code> count; the run summary provides detailed trial-level outcomes.</p></div><p>Local <code>_evalkit-sandbox/</code> directories retain candidate <em>and evaluator</em> files. Review them for sensitive data before sharing. The dashboard lets you navigate runs, trial timelines, and candidate files without digging through JSON.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#read-and-process-results">Reading and processing results →</a></p></section>

      <section id="configure" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>07 / CONFIGURE A PROJECT</span><h2>Discover evals by ID.</h2></div><p>The starter uses an explicit <code>src/registry.ts</code>. For a config-based project, default-export each <code>defineEval(...)</code> from <code>evals/*.eval.ts</code> (for the example above, add <code>export default greetingEval</code>). Evalkit imports matching files in sorted order. Configure discovery and a model matrix in <code>evalkit.config.js</code>:</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">evalkit.config.js</div><Code code={projectConfig} lang="js" theme="github-light" /></div><p>Paths, including fixtures and report directories, resolve relative to the config. You can customize <code>include</code>, <code>exclude</code>, and <code>testDir</code>, or supply an explicit <code>registry</code> in the config. Run from that project directory. The starter has no config, so it continues to load its registry.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#configuration-and-discovery">Project configuration and discovery →</a></p></section>

      <section id="matrices" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>08 / SWEEP PARAMETERS</span><h2>Test more than one setting.</h2></div><p>A matrix lazily expands evals × parameter axes into independent cells; <code>--eval</code> selects a human eval ID and <code>--model</code> narrows an axis. Inspect the count with <code>--dry-run</code> before running. Plans above <code>execution.maxCells</code> require <code>--all</code>.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">TERMINAL · from the configured project</div><Code code={matrixCommands} lang="bash" theme="github-light" /></div><p>The adapter must read <code>context.parameters.model</code> to apply the selected model; the included <code>piAgent()</code> does not do this automatically. Cell keys and effective parameters are saved with the reports.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#run-from-the-cli">Matrices, filters, and safety limits →</a></p></section>

      <section id="next" class="doc-section doc-last"><div class="doc-heading"><span>09 / WHAT’S NEXT</span><h2>Go further.</h2></div><p>Read the <a href="/docs/manual/">full manual ↗</a> for suites, parameter matrices, dynamic fixtures, dashboard controls, and result processing. The code in <a href="https://github.com/leostera/evalkit/tree/main/examples/starter">examples/starter ↗</a> is a runnable reference.</p><p>Today, only <code>user(...)</code> transcript steps and <code>predicate(...)</code> scorers execute. Judge scorers, remote Agents SDK transport, enforced timeouts, and project scaffolding are still in progress. Evalkit’s packages are workspace-local for now.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="https://github.com/leostera/evalkit">Explore the repository <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Site>
